eed by the party leaders who have  met the speaker  they can actually appoint the speaker of  parliament as the interim president but  he has to win a lot of confidence in  parliament which is simply a simple  majority which is not difficult task for  him to get  and then he will have to ask another np  to form a government and that government  we have to carry on for six months uh  providing relief to the members of the  public and putting laying down the basic  requirements to  to  to eliminate the current economic crisis  and also to enable the country to have  another general election after six  months time  which would enable the country to elect  its own government so this is the  timeline and the strategy that is being  proposed by many people who have been  engaged in the protest campaign now  the thing is though even if the  government is replaced there's no  short-term solution to fix the economic  woes of sri lanka is there and sri  lankans are really facing fuel and food  shortages for some time yet  well you are perfectly correct  simply because we have an interim  government  of all political parties doesn't mean  that sri lanka would be able to resolve  all the acute economic issues that we  are facing today  specifically we don't have any foreign  exchange with the central bank of sri  lanka and as a result sri lanka is  unable to maintain the minimum import  program  which includes the importation of fuel  cooking gas  medicines food items  inputs for industries and so forth so  there will be a very difficult time  period for sri lankans in the next two  to three months time period  but what it means is that given the  complexity and the acuteness of the  current economic crisis  my guess is that all of us will have to  make the supreme sacrifice  we'll have to  actually change our present modern  lifestyles we have to change we have to  become you know very altruistic people  in the country we have to work for  nothing so likewise lots of demands are  to be made of the people in sri lanka  to enable the country to come out of the  present crisis  you're in colombo at the moment what's  it like personally living there how can  you carry out your daily tasks are you  able to go to the petrol station are you  able to get the food that you need  well of course i stood in the petrol  cave for four days  in order to get a percent experience of  you know what it feels like  three days it was in vain because the  bosses didn't come with petrol on the  fourth day i put my vehicle to the queue  at three o'clock in the morning but the  bowser came only at nine o'clock in the  night and by the time i went home it was  around 11 o'clock but i had lots of  experience with the people who stood  there in the cube and the agony and the  difficulties that these people are going  through i actually experienced myself  right now i don't use my car because i  was given only a 10 to 20 liters of  petrol which is not sufficient for me to  use my car i have kept it as a reserve  for an emergency i either walk or use  public transport whenever they are  available to move from one place to  another now these are the type of life  that we are living in sri lanka today in  color  well we really appreciate your insights  thank you very much wa wiji wadena an  independent economic analyst thank you  for your time

